<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch11-section-ageostrophic-wind-derivation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Deriving the ageostrophic wind</title>

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis" text="custom">Chapter 8</xref>
    that the horizontal acceleration of air parcels making up the wind<mdash />given by
    <m>\dfrac{Du}{Dt}</m> in the zonal direction and
    <m>\dfrac{Dv}{Dt}</m> in the meridional direction<mdash />has the next-largest magnitude
    for mid-latitude synoptic scale analysis of the horizontal equations of motion: <m>\mathcal{O}(10^{-4} \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2})</m>.
    Although this horizontal Lagrangian acceleration is about 10 times smaller than the horizontal pressure gradient and Coriolis accelerations
    for mid-latitude synoptic scale motions,
    retaining it allows for prediction and explanation of large-scale vertical motions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider the horizontal equations of motion in height coordinates with these three largest accelerations retained:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-x" number="yes">
      \frac{Du}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x} + fv
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-y" number="yes">
      \frac{Dv}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y} - fu
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the Coriolis parameter <m>f = 2 \Omega \sin{\phi}</m> has been substituted for simplicity.
    These equations can be combined into vector form as
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-vector" number="yes">
      \frac{D \vec{v}}{Dt} = -\frac{1}{\rho} \nabla_z p - f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the "z" subscript emphasizes height coordinates are used and pressure is measured at constant height.
    These are the <term>quasi-geostrophic horizontal equations of motion</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the equations of geostrophic balance,
    which are <em>diagnostic</em>,
    the equations of quasi-geostrophic horizontal motion given by
    <xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-x" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-vector" text="type-global" />
    are <em>prognostic</em> because they feature time derivatives through inclusion of the horizontal Lagrangian acceleration.
    While the equations of quasi-geostrophic motion are more accurate to real atmospheric motions than the equations of geostrophic balance
    because they retain the horizontal Lagrangian acceleration,
    they still are approximate since smaller accelerations such as the curvature terms and viscous friction acceleration are neglected.
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-geostrophic-balance" text="custom">Chapter 8</xref>
    that the zonal and meridional components of the geostrophic wind are given in height coordinates by
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x-reproduced2" number="yes">
      u_g = -\frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    and
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-y-reproduced2" number="yes">
      v_g = \frac{1}{\rho f} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    respectively, and its vector equation is given by
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-reproduced2" number="yes">
      \vec{v}_g = \frac{1}{\rho f} \, \hat{k} \times \nabla_z p
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x-reproduced2" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-reproduced2" text="type-global" />
    each can be rearranged to isolate the horizontal pressure gradient acceleration
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-x-reproduced2-rearranged" number="yes">
      f u_g = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-y-reproduced2-rearranged" number="yes">
      -f v_g = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
    </md>
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-reproduced2-rearranged" number="yes">
      f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}_g = -\frac{1}{\rho} \nabla_z p
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    for substitution into
    <xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-x" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-quasi-geostrophic-vector" text="type-global" />:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x-start" number="yes">
      \frac{Du}{Dt} = -f v_g + fv = f(v - v_g)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-y-start" number="yes">
      \frac{Dv}{Dt} = f u_g - fu = -f(u - u_g)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-start" number="yes">
      \frac{D \vec{v}}{Dt}
      =
      f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}_g - f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}
      =
      -f \, \hat{k} \times (\vec{v} - \vec{v}_g)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Note the vector identity
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-cross-product-identity" number="yes">
      \hat{k} \times (\hat{k} \times \vec{A}) = -\vec{A}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    was used to produce <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-wind-vector-reproduced2-rearranged" text="tyep-global" />.
  </p>

  <p>
    We define the <term>ageostrophic wind</term> as
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-basic" number="yes">
      \vec{v}_a
      = u_a \, \hat{i} + v_a \, \hat{j}
      = \vec{v} - \vec{v}_g
      = (u - u_g) \, \hat{i} + (v - v_g) \, \hat{j}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where <m>\vec{v} = u \, \hat{i} + v \, \hat{j}</m> is the true horizontal wind.
    <xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-basic" text="type-global" /> makes it clear that
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x-basic" number="yes">
      u_a = u - u_g
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-y-basic" number="yes">
      v_a = v - v_g
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-basic-reproduced" number="yes">
      \vec{v}_a = \vec{v} - \vec{v}_g
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    In other words,
    the ageostrophic wind is the deviation of the true horizontal wind from geostrophy,
    and it results from imbalance between the horizontal pressure gradient force and the horizontal Coriolis force.
  </p>

  <p>
    Substituting
    <xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x-basic" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-basic-reproduced" text="type-global" />
    into
    <xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x-start" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-start" text="type-global" />
    produces
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x-intermediate" number="yes">
      \frac{Du}{Dt} = f v_a
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-y-intermediate" number="yes">
      \frac{Dv}{Dt} = -f u_a
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector-intermediate" number="yes">
      \frac{D \vec{v}}{Dt}
      = -f \, \hat{k} \times \vec{v}_a
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-x" number="yes">
      u_a = u - u_g = -\frac{1}{f} \frac{Dv}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-y" number="yes">
      v_a = v - v_g = \frac{1}{f} \frac{Du}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector" number="yes">
      \vec{v}_a = \vec{v} - \vec{v}_g
      = \frac{1}{f} \, \hat{k} \times \frac{D\vec{v}}{Dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    where the vector identity given by <xref ref="eqn-cross-product-identity" text="type-global" />
    again was used to produce <xref ref="eqn-ageostrophic-wind-vector" text="type-global" />.
  </p>

</section>